WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:02.680
I have an update.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:05.560
NASA has said that there will
be a hardware random number

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.240
generator on the rover
that we have access to.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.040
And after that, they
made one more comment.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.120
They reminded me that we
just need our algorithm

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.170
to work in practice.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.780
For something to
work in practice,

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.720
it means that there's always
some possibility of error,

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.180
but perhaps the
possibility is so small

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.080
that we ignore it in practice.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.050
And if this sounds
crazy, just realize

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.560
that in the physical world,
nothing is ever certain.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.500
There is always
chances for error.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.720
For example, chip packaging
contains small amounts

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.120
of radioactive contaminants,
and when these decay,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.130
they release alpha
particles, which can actually

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:51.410
flip bits in memory and perhaps
change a number unexpectedly.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:53.960
And even more
interesting, cosmic rays

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.990
can also cause soft errors.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.980
We can never remove the
chance of error completely.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:04.390
And I asked NASA, exactly,
well, what error of probability

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:06.024
is acceptable?

00:01:06.024 --> 00:01:07.690
And they said, we
just need to make sure

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:11.630
that the probability of
error for a given trial

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:16.230
is less than the odds of hitting
the lottery twice in a row.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:17.730
And the odds of
hitting the lottery,

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:22.510
say 649, twice in a row, is 6
times 10 to the negative 14.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:25.720
So let's just be safe and
round down, and say our error

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.780
probability is 10
to the minus 15.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.010
Safe enough.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.280
We will not expect
to see an error,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.300
and it could run hundreds
or thousands of times.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.600
Now, my question is,
would access to randomness

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.670
help us speed up a
decision algorithm,

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.110
such as this primality test?

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:45.380
And this is a deep question.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.650
So let's step back and
look at the big picture.

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:52.210
Given some collection of
integers from 1 to some limit,

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:56.760
n, say, let's think of it
as our universe of integers.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.090
We can always divide a
collection into two sets.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.440
And a decision
problem can actually

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.250
be thought of as a
partition into two sets.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:12.460
For example, provided
some n is n less than 100,

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:16.420
it will output yes for all
integers less than 100.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.590
So here is one collection.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:22.920
And no for all others,
which is another collection.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.050
OK, so let's now focus
on recognizing primes

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:28.390
with the decision.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:31.310
And ideally what we would
like is some simply computed

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:37.060
criteria that all primes satisfy
and no composites satisfy.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.820
Now if we knew some
simple pattern which

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.140
describes the location
of all primes,

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.980
and only primes, then
given some number n,

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.890
we could simply check if
n follows that pattern.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:55.060
But the problem is that so
far, no exhaustive and simply

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.660
computed pattern has
been found for all primes

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.885
and no composites, or all
composites and no primes.

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:15.930
But we do know fast tests
for most composite numbers.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:20.380
So, for example, a
simply computed criteria

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.670
would be a test for evenness.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:27.670
And all even numbers
are divisible by 2.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:30.400
It's fast, because we can tell
if something's even by just

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.420
looking at the last digit.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:35.400
And even as n grows, the
problem doesn't get any harder.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:37.740
We just look at that
last digit, also known

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.160
as the low-order bit.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.590
Now realize that we can
use this evenness test

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:49.210
as a low-quality
composite test, in that,

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:52.940
we could have some algorithm
accept an integer, n,

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:56.120
and all our algorithm does
is check if it's even.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:00.510
If it is even and greater
than 2, then we know,

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.990
with 100% certainty,
it is composite,

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:05.990
because we have proof.

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:09.240
Think of 2 as our
composite witness.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.590
However, if not, then
we aren't exactly sure.

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:16.329
If it's odd, it could be prime,
since all primes are odd,

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:19.250
or it could be one of the many
composites which are odds,

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.769
such as 9 or 15.

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:25.030
And this massive region
of odd composites

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.160
makes for an unacceptable test.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.110
Now, to be clear,
let's draw this.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:33.110
Provided some n, n can be
either prime or composite.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:37.260
If n is prime, our algorithm
will say prime 100%

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:41.080
of the time, since no primes are
even that are greater than 2.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:44.540
And it will never say composite
when a prime is provided.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:47.300
However, if n is
composite, our algorithm

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:51.500
will say composite about 50%
of the time and prime 50%

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.220
of the time.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:55.560
So this means that if our
algorithm outputs composite,

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.015
it means it found proof,
a composite witness.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.090
However, if our
algorithm outputs prime,

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:11.590
we know there is an unacceptably
large chance of error.

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:13.910
Up until now our
strategy has dealt

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:19.720
with this unacceptably
large error by iteration.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:22.410
And let's draw the
flow of our machine.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:26.220
Given some n, our
machine does a series

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:30.970
of divisibility tests
starting at a is 2.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:33.570
It asks, does a divide n?

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.250
And if it doesn't
divide n, then we

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:38.855
can eliminate all
of this region.

00:05:38.855 --> 00:05:42.310
Then it asks the next
question, does n divide 3?

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:45.620
And if not, then we
eliminate that whole section.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.590
And does n divide
5, 7, and so on.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:53.390
And notice these are disjoint
sets which gradually fill up

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:55.670
all possible composites.

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:58.160
Now if we ever answer
yes along the way,

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:01.240
then we have proof
that n is composite.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:04.650
And a, whatever it is at
that point, is our witness.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:08.040
So we halt an output
composite from our algorithm.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.020
Otherwise, we continue
trying until we exhaust

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:15.010
all possible composites, until
we hit the square root of n

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:17.150
since we know, remember,
every composite number

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:21.020
n must have a factor less than
or equal to the square root

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:22.230
of n.

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.400
So this really leads
to the final question

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:25.500
in our algorithm.

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:29.460
If no witnesses are
found, and a is greater

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.880
than the square root of n,
then we suddenly have proof,

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.360
and we halt an output prime.

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:43.580
So realize we have two exit
paths through our algorithm.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:46.440
Both paths represent
proof of certainty

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.870
that n is either
prime or composite.

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:53.410
And when n is prime, we
try all possible divisors,

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:55.460
and we basically
rule them all out,

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:57.950
and that is our proof
that n is prime.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.030
The problem with
this strategy is

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:03.230
that our test value
a, at minimum,

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.750
requires us to cycle through
every prime starting from 2

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:08.240
to the square root of n.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:12.180
So as n grows, the number
of primes grow with it.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:15.590
It results in too many
iterations in the worst case,

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:18.860
such as when we provide
a prime to our algorithm.

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:20.590
Looking at the big
picture, now, realize

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:23.720
it's providing proof
when n is prime,

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:26.930
which we now know we
don't exactly need.

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:29.210
No one said we
needed to prove it.

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:36.520
We just needed to be 99.9999,
15 9's percent certain.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:41.270
So we actually need to think
about the composite test that's

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:42.940
being used in our algorithm.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:46.900
Remember, our fastest trial
division primality tests,

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:53.040
thus far, have tried to use
prime patterns, such 6k,

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.110
or all primes are of the
form 6k plus or minus

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:58.940
1, to help walk along
the primes only,

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:02.170
and eliminate many of the
composites to save time.

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:04.180
And remember, a
test like this can

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:06.400
be turned into a composite test.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.910
That is, given some integer n is
of the form 6k plus or minus 1,

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:15.170
then we could say
it's probably prime.

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:16.760
But there are many
composites, still,

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.930
which follow this pattern.

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:23.420
It doesn't include primes
only, just all primes

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:24.770
and some composites.

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:27.850
And we can think of these
extra composites as a leak.

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:31.080
And this leak is
our source of error.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.549
Now, if we invert it and
ask, is n not of the form 6k

00:08:34.549 --> 00:08:39.809
plus or minus 1, then we
can say with 100% certainty

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:41.120
it is composite.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:44.920
So the leak is our source
of error on the prime side.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.590
But in this case,
it's unacceptable.

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:49.360
It's not a non-negligible error.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.370
There's a much
larger probability.

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:53.590
We need to define a
new composite testing

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:57.290
procedure which is able
to shrink this space

00:08:57.290 --> 00:09:01.350
and make the chance of error
negligible, which means we'll

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:05.000
need to review how we can
actually shrink errors

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:06.480
with iterations.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.910
I think we should now post
our ideas below regarding,

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:14.920
what kind of tests might
we want to perform?

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:20.140
And also, more importantly, how
could a random number generator

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:22.258
really help us?

